  {
    "episode_id": 42598,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42598.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "We thank our veterans, we honor them, and we thank our first responders. I want to talk today about a change in CDC guidance that they have issued in how we report the coronavirus statistics. I learned about this this morning, and Dr. Acton and I have talked about it, discussed it, and we really both feel that while we will follow the guidance and certainly understand the guidance, we want to make sure that we show data in a way that is very transparent to the people of the state of Ohio. And in a way that does allow people to make apples to apples type comparisons. So we're going to be reporting this in more detail actually than the new guidance requires. So let me kind of walk through about how this is going to work and Dr. Acton. when she talks and gives her statistics will explain it as well. As a lay person, I'm going to give it a shot here. So the numbers that you will see today, I believe, will be under this new reporting system. On April 5th of this year, the Council of State and Territorial Epidemiologists, known as the CSTE, put forth new case surveillance recommendations. The CSTE is a national organization made up of member states and territories representing public health epidemiologists, scientists who study infectious diseases and their spread. The CSTE provides technical guidance and assistance to the states and to the federal partners, such as the Centers for Disease Control, the CDC. CDC is now recommended that states follow these. Up until now, before this change, the guidance allowed doctors to count as COVID-19 cases only those that had been confirmed by a laboratory test. Only those that had been confirmed by a laboratory test. So when you saw the numbers that Dr. Acton was reporting, that was only those cases. The new guidance was developed by a committee of state epidemiologists from Idaho, Alabama, Iowa, Hawaii, Michigan and is now a formal recommendation from the Centers for Disease Control. It will include cases that meet the following criteria. Let me just say this and I'll say it again. We're going to be reporting this The old numbers, what would have been the old numbers, the old method of coming up with these numbers, we're gonna then report at the same time what comes under the new numbers, then we're gonna put the total. So those, anybody looking at those will be able to see that. And I wanna go through and explain what the new number that you're going to be seeing there every day, what that actually represents. Quick tests. Let me start with quick tests that have determined the presence of COVID-19 antibodies in the blood. These are the rapid tests that will soon be coming to Ohio, where doctors can prick your finger, check your blood for antibodies, and have a result within minutes. Under the old guidelines, Under the old guidelines, if they had not been changed, those tests would not have been reported positive. You would not have seen those numbers. So this is evolving. The science is evolving. The way of counting is evolving as well. So we would not have been allowed to count people or would not have been told to count people who test positive for antibodies that they had not, if they had not previously had a lab test confirming the presence of COVID-19. So this new method of testing will show up in the second number. And without this new guidance, it would not have shown up at all. Again, a new way of testing. This new number will also include the following. A person will be counted if there is clinical evidence and epidemiological evidence of the presence of COVID-19 and when there is no other likely diagnosis, even if there is no laboratory test. So in this case, no laboratory test, but there's clinical evidence and epidemiological evidence of the presence of COVID-19, and there's no other likely diagnosis. That will be the standard that the reporters will use. The reporters, the medical professional people. This means that doctors in hospitals or outpatient facilities may determine that someone has COVID-19 based on his or her symptoms, such as fever, cough, pneumonia, along with a history of possible exposure to a confirmed case. An example might be a person who had all of those symptoms, who was living in a nursing home that already had confirmed COVID-19 patients. Now, as we report our data moving forward for deaths, we'll have the following numbers. Confirmed deaths, which is under the old category, new CDC guidance deaths, and then we draw a line and we'll add the two together. By the way, the National Center for Health Statistics, which is part of the Centers for Disease Control in their death reporting, for COVID-19, as my understanding is now reporting probable deaths in those numbers. So what this new guidance enables all of us to do is to better track who has the virus currently and those who have had the virus, those who are recovered, those who are no longer a threat in passing it along to others. Using this guidance will be instrumental in helping us trace the spread of the disease and eventually isolate it to smaller pockets or areas so we can open things back up again. At the same time, it will allow us to target limited resources for a better public health response. So again, the way we are going to report this data is going to enable people to compare these numbers as they change, but people are going to know what each set of numbers actually represents. We think that for Ohio citizens, this is the best way to report this. We are going to follow what the CDC has said, and we will do that, but we will break those numbers out. Let me talk about some good news. We have this announcement. There are many Ohioans we've talked about who are on the front line who are really making a difference. Ohio food banks continue to provide Ohioans, Fran talked about the food banks with the food and personal care items that they need. As I mentioned yesterday, Ohio companies are coming up with innovative ways to meet the needs of those working on the front lines of this crisis. And every day we're going to try to mention maybe one or two of these companies that are really making a difference. We had a list yesterday of the manufacturers and we saw some of the things that they were doing. One of these groups is our distilleries. Who knew, huh? Our distilleries who have stepped up and are now producing large quantities of hand sanitizer. Jobs Ohio has purchased more than 3,100 cases, tilling more than 1 million ounces of much-needed hand sanitizer from our Ohio distillers, distillaries, and we'll be donating it to the Ohio Association of Food Banks. Shipments of the hand sanitizer are arriving at distribution centers this week and will be delivered to Ohio's 12 food banks next week and over the coming months as they need it. So we thank them for that. We want to thank the distillers and Jobs Ohio for helping the Ohio Association of Food Banks as they work to provide Ohio families with food and the other personal health care items that they need to stay healthy. Yesterday, I got a question about our food trucks. Food trucks, I know, are certainly a favorite of many of my grandchildren, our grandchildren. And there can be quite a hit and provide some really, really great food. The question yesterday was about food, these food trucks at our rest areas and how this would help People who are driving, people who have to be driving, and certainly how it would help our truck drivers. Our rest area certainly are not as crowded as they have been in the past. But it is important that our truck drivers and other essential personnel have access to hot meals as they get food and other essential items to our grocery stores. Truckers give a shout out to them. They're making a difference. certainly are essential. Last week the Federal Highway Administration announced they would temporarily allow us to use these food trucks. Our Ohio Department of Transportation has created a new permit which will allow food vendors to sell food to truck drivers and other essential personnel stopping at the state's 86 rest areas. The permit is of no cost to the vendor and can be found on ODOT's website. And let me just give that is transportation.ohile.gov, transportation.ohile.gov. This is in addition to the regular vending machine service that has been provided for many years by opportunities for Ohioans with disabilities. Let me talk for a moment about Medicaid and some changes. Our goal in Ohio is to keep as many people as healthy as possible and to ensure those that get sick with COVID-19 that they get the treatment that they need. That certainly includes reducing barriers to care. Medicaid, the managed care plans, and my care of Ohio plans have come together to remove some barriers for the members. Those receiving Medicaid benefits during this difficult crisis. They have collaborated to maintain the health of individuals to keep them out of the hospital and reduce undue strain on our health care system. These groups are trying to reduce the administrative requirements of physicians and others to give them more flexibility so that they can focus on safe patient care. More details will be available on coronavirus.ohile.gov or by calling COVID-19 call center at 1-833-4-ASK-ODH. But let me also give an overview of some of these changes. Medicaid has eased several pharmacy benefit restrictions as follows. Prior authorization will be bypassed for new prescriptions. Members will receive pharmacy benefits regardless of in-network or out-of-network provider status. Third, the threshold for refills on certain prescriptions will be relaxed. Number four, pharmacists who dispense emergency refills without a prescription will be reimbursed, number five, pharmacies that dispense over-the-counter medication without a prescription will also be reimbursed. Member copays, member copays will be waived. This is for all prescriptions, and 90-day supplies on maintenance medications will be authorized. The managed care plans have already lifted prior authorization for all but a few services. This took effect on March 27th. Physicians and other providers without prior authorization by a managed care plan are using clinical judgment to determine if something is a medical necessity. Again, for further details, please check coronavirus.ohio.gov, coronavirus.ohio.gov or call 1-833-4-ASK-ODH for more information. Let me now turn it over to the Lieutenant Governor, John.",
