    "turn_text": "that's still pretty good for a seventh grader and I really appreciate Katie and Pam Meadows or teacher and all the teachers out there who really are stepping up to make sure that education goes on and using even a difficult time period as a way to teach a new skill and to help all of us get through this. So thank you very much. to Pam Meadows and to all the teachers out there that are doing great work. One of the things Governor and I do calls with mayors and business leaders and county commissioners and elected officials all the time and one of them that I did this morning was with the transit authorities and the mayors of the cities where those transit authorities are operating. What we did working with Dr. Acton's Department of Health was to set out some protocols and some guidance on how they could make sure that the transit operations were as safe as they possibly could be for the people operating the transit authority, for the people who are riding on those buses or trains or the various tools that we have available for them. The one thing that the leaders of the transit authorities wanted me to remind folks is that the only people that are supposed to be out there are people who are doing essential business. And so make sure that if you're using the transit system that you're out there doing essential business, there's no reason to bring people together in those spaces unnecessarily. And they wanted me to emphasize that as a reminder to take into consideration the safety of the workers who are operating those and to be considerate to them as you're doing this. They're asking their customers to wear a mask. If you have masks available and you're on the bus, they would like for you to wear a mask. We're working to provide all of the gear, the PPE that the bus drivers and the transit operators need. But they really did ask for the benefit of you, the other riders and the people who work there that you use safe hygiene habits only ride when necessary and that you wear a mask. if at all possible. So remember we're all in this together and to minimize the threat of spread, we can all take those precautions that help out. Also, really appreciate the interaction the governor and I have had with businesses where we're hearing what your your thoughts are during this time had a great call with businesses this morning where they shared with me some of the some of the things that they're doing as essential businesses to keep their workplaces clean also offering feedback that as we look to the future how all businesses can do these kinds of things and And how we need to source that and communicate that out there for the future. So we really appreciate the dialogue that we're having with you out there who are the essential businesses and the advice that you're providing back to us on how we can prepare that future where we're all being thoughtful and making sure we're taking the precautions to ensure that we don't have a reoccurrence or expand the spread as we look at these things down the road. Also, I got a call. had a couple of them from farmers saying, are there restrictions on us under the essential business list? Goodness no. Farmers are essential. We need you out there to plant your crops and to make sure that we are prepared to keep the food supply chain humming. We are grateful for what you do. But in all circumstances, whether you're a farmer, manufacturer, whatever setting you're in, please practice safe workplace conditions. And we thank all the farmers out there and everybody in that food supply chain that are doing what they need to make us to keep us well fed and well supplied as we get through this difficult time period in our state's history. And finally. job postings for these essential businesses. We now have 614 employers who are not only essential, but we describe them as critical to what we're doing to keep things humming in our state. There are 38,000 jobs that have been posted out there from health care facilities, manufacturers, food supply chain of workers that they need, people to go to work to help keep this going. And that is at coronavirus.ohio.gov. So, over this weekend, if you have some time and you might be able to contribute to the workforce, please do so. Please go there and look at the jobs that are available. We would really value everybody who can participate in helping these businesses if you could please do so. So, on this good Friday, I wish you all a peaceful weekend and we thank We thank you for the great job you're doing out across the state. We're flattening the curve, thanks to you, and God bless you all. Governor?",
