  {
    "episode_id": 42598,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42598.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 36,
    "turn_text": "Well, the good news is there is guidance out there for essential workers. And that is actually expanding. It was originally intended for hospitals and nursing homes. But that is being expanded to other essential businesses. And my understanding is it will even be to things like grocery stores. But the guidance is subtle. Our hospitals haven't had to enact it fully yet. But if there is a situation where the healthcare workers, you know, they're so overrun, they actually have guidance on clinical. So without the test, and that is the guidance I've mentioned before, that was you have to have had three days without a fever, without any, you know, Tylenol or Advil or anything falsely keeping it down. And you have to have been seven days at least from the onset of symptoms. And that really lets us know that your risk of infection is much less. And then there's also guidance about the PPE they're wearing to make sure then that they're not spreading. So there is some guidance that is especially for essential businesses and that's being expanded. And then that guidance, as you can imagine the future, and we'll be talking next week a lot more about the kinds of things we'll need to sort of kind of gradually responsibly start to move our world back to something that won't look exactly like normal until we have a vaccine, but certainly not what we're experiencing now. And that guidance will really continue to evolve and spread out. Thank you.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_20",
