  {
    "episode_id": 42598,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42598.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 39,
    "turn_text": "Well, no one is safe from the coronavirus. What has happened is we were able, the director was able to, and her team, keep the virus out. Once the virus comes in, obviously, in any kind of congregate setting, it's very dangerous. We were very concerned about this. We have set up a special group to work on this. The director, I went through the other day and outlined all the things that director had done. They started taking temperature of members of the staff who were coming in, making sure anybody went home, who had a high temperature, anybody who gave any symptoms. been following all the right protocol. Now what we have done in the last few days is we're starting to surge in there more testing. So we will be completely testing some of these prisons, every prisoner, and that will much better able to determine who has it, who doesn't have it, how to segregate them, and how really to attack this. So new testing, widespread testing in any prison where the coronavirus, we have evidence that coronavirus is there. The other thing that we're doing as far as the personal protection equipment, we are that equipment in there as well. So this is something I take very seriously. We have a responsibility to the employees, TRC employees. We have a responsibility to the prisoners who are in our care and we will do everything that we can to try to keep them safe. We've also, as you know, started a procedure where some of the prisoners will be released early. waiting for the legislature will be meeting shortly the committee to make decisions in regard to that. So we are also looking at other potential inmates as far as the ability to possibly release them. So that is a work in progress. But whenever you have a congregate setting, we worry very, very much about it. And so our focus now as we get more mask in as we get more equipment in. We're going to focus that on wherever we find a hot spot where that hot spot is in a nursing home or that hot spot is in a prison. These are things that keep us awake at night. These are things that we worry about and we've we are focused on it. That does not mean it won't spread, does not mean we won't have more trouble. But we're doing absolutely everything that we can with the tools that we have and we're trying to get, you know, we've been pulling additional tools in. So on the COVID-19 mass, for example, some of those are directly going into the prison.",
