  {
    "episode_id": 42598,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42598.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think the answer is probably yes. But I think it's always a balance. And it's all, you weigh these things out. Fran was telling me that she watched a show on the polio vaccine, something that we remember when we were kids and got shots, and that that was rolled out pretty quickly. And there were some people concerned about that. But judgments had to be made. And they made the judgment then, instead of waiting five years or whatever it would have been, we need to get this out. And it turned out many, many lives, obviously, were saved because of that. So I think it's always a balance. I don't think there's any, you know, it's hard sometimes to weigh those things out. You know, what we did with the FDA, for example, in regard to the tell and the sterilization process, they checked all the boxes. They went through everything. They just crammed it together in a shorter period of time. But FDA, you know, would have no business authorizing that. procedure unless they were sure it was okay. So I think you're seeing things when there's an emergency human nature is you can get stuff done faster and sometimes that's just the way human nature is. So I think, but I think it's always a balance and we always have to be careful. We don't want to shove things through too quickly, but we're in a hurry because we got lives at stake.",
