  {
    "episode_id": 42598,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42598.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 77,
    "turn_text": "Well, sure. Look, I understand what they're saying. But if you, no one should doubt how deadly this is. You looked at the news last night or this morning and the coffins and being buried in New York. if you recall what we saw on the news coming out of Italy. And despite the fact that when we go outside today, looks like just another spring day, we have your fellow highlands who are dying today. You have fellow highlands who are in intensive care. You have fellow highlands who are not able to breathe. So this is deadly. This is not a game. This is not something that, you know, I woke up someday and decided I'm going to, you know, impose these regulations on the state. Well, highlands have done well. And when you talk about the modeling They're projections of what might happen if certain things happen. Ohioans changed the outcome. And what we did is we did stay home by and large. We stopped school. We did many, many things that were uncomfortable in doing, and we continue to do those. If we had not done that, we would be right now in the midst, heading right into the midst of what you saw on TV in Italy, what we've seen in New York. There's a reason that Ohio's numbers are a lot better than we projected, and a lot better, frankly, than other places. And that's because Ohio did this early. We were strong. and that made all the difference in the world. There are different alternative futures, and it depends on what you do today and what we do today. But no one should think that this thing is over. Any Ohio who has not had this can get it. And there's some evidence that even people who've had it might be able to get it. I'll leave that to the scientists. So while we're going to work, one of the things we're working on this weekend, besides our budget, we're working on this weekend is how we get back in. That's not going to happen overnight and it's not going to happen by flipping a switch and everything's going to be back to normal. I wish I could do that. I wish the reality of life was that. For those who doubt that, I just say please be careful. It is out there. It is still among us. It is still killing your fellow Ohioans. And what we need to do is stay home. We need this weekend when families normally celebrate. We have to resist that poll and we have to celebrate virtually in any way that we can. Please be careful.",
