  {
    "episode_id": 42598,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42598.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 80,
    "turn_text": "I think there I'd have to take a look at what you're talking about because that I think the unmitigated part it would have peaked sooner Actually, maybe that that is what you're talking about the one curve. So if we had done nothing We would have seen a huge spike. I mean that that is the whole point and that's what would have spiked Quickly on our hospitals. It's what we saw happen in Washington originally we see that and you know, even though New York had a little time to prepare it, they didn't have as much time as I think they would have liked. And so in Wuhan, it was the ultimate of that. They had no time to prepare. So that first curve is really You get it over with. You'd have more people get infected quickly. You'd have far more deaths. And a hospital system that would have led to even more excess deaths because people wouldn't be able to get the care they need, which is probably happening in New York right now for the other things they really need to get in there. So there's so many confounding factors. By stretching it out, it goes longer.",
