  {
    "episode_id": 42598,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42598.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 82,
    "turn_text": "There's no doubt about that. It's a controlled burn versus a wild burn. We're trying to like slow burn it and you're trying to find that sweet spot where you keep everything functioning. But the bottom line is it's not just about the beds in the hospital right now. We don't have gear for routine surgeries. We have a shortage of medicines. we're using with ventilators. So it's a complicated scenario. It made the peak go out farther and we're so flat. We're actually, when we say peak, we're not going to look very peaky. We're sort of a plateau, if that makes sense. And on this mountain, and then it will be a slower decline. But with each and every day we buy, we're more prepared in all our systems. And with each and every day we buy, there could be more PPE, there could be more testing, and it makes us better able to protect people and stop the spread. So this is the nature of a pandemic curve.",
