  {
    "episode_id": 42598,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/2/42598.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 91,
    "turn_text": "Look, we're always seeking as much information as we can. What you're looking for is whatever the trends are. And so, you know, you're going to establish with the new numbers, the new category, you will establish in a few days where there's numbers of what they look like, which way they're moving. And then you go from there and see where it goes from there. You're going to be doing the same thing on the numbers we're already counting, the way we're already counting them. So I think you're going to be looking at both, what you're really looking for always is trends, which way is that trend line moving. And as Dr. Atkins just said, going into this, it would appear that we're maybe going up a little bit, but we've really slowed that down. And so we may be at a plateau and then start back down. So I would not think that the new numbers would, when we can take those in action with the old numbers and look at both of them at the same time, I would be surprised if they tell us something different. But we'll have to see and kind of judge that. You see the great irony, for example, of New York on the news at night, and they're celebrating that they've slowed this thing down, and yet it's a huge, huge day for deaths, and then we have the same thing. I mean, we kind of blow by this, but we have people who are dying right now in Ohio. And we celebrate the fact that we're not going up like a rocket, but we have to mourn the fact that we're still, we're losing people and that will unfortunately continue for a while.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_12",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
