        "text": "The modeling numbers will continue to adjust based on social distancing effectiveness and virus spread rate.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Most reported cases are not confirmed by testing.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Many cases are diagnosed clinically based on symptoms alone.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The modeling projections are not precise to a specific point in time.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Unconfirmed cases often remain at home rather than seeking medical care.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "We will revisit the modeling curve once we have sufficient data.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "At least 80% of current cases are untested.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Some nursing home cases are not tested but are still presumed infected.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "In some nursing homes, a few tested individuals lead to assumptions about widespread infection.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Accurate case reporting depends on reliable testing infrastructure.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Public health models require ongoing recalibration to reflect real-world behavior and transmission dynamics.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a significant undercounting of actual infections due to limited testing capacity.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Clinical diagnosis based on symptoms is a valid but imperfect method for identifying infections.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Health authorities rely on extrapolation and inference when testing coverage is incomplete.",
